#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75d27dfc-1297-5045-bedd-10e171209430" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Using the Distributive Law"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4de44592-363f-53b0-9452-63f02463612d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de44592-363f-53b0-9452-63f02463612d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256528} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ffd4e0-bfb9-5b97-9e69-8e43ad3307ba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "486b29b8-ce2e-59f6-a2b3-65c777f54464" :question/math-academy-id "q-101625" :question/problem "If $p+2=3(p-3)+6,$ then $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand-side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3(p-3)+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3\\cdot p-3\\cdot 3+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-9+6 \\\\ p+2 & =3p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must now bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side. \n\nNotice that we have $p$ on the left-hand side and $3p$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the right-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side. \n\nThe first step is to remove the $p$ from the left-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting $p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =3p-3 \\\\ p+2-p & =3p-3-p \\\\ 2 & =2p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-3$ from the right-hand side. We do this by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2p-3 \\\\ 2+3 & =2p-3+3 \\\\ 5 & =2p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{2p}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}p}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ceb6c84-a16b-5519-abd6-d2bb72ea81d3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Set Corresponding to a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a31d182b-1683-5fb8-be8c-2fa373c3d8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9,11,13,…}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31d182b-1683-5fb8-be8c-2fa373c3d8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9,11,13,…}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540} :answer-field/id #uuid "65dc58dd-a0d2-5b42-99f2-71db2dabf621" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46e08706-7515-5d4c-aacf-8fa37890b6f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-101992" :question/problem "Which set represents all odd integers greater than $8?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To express the desired set, we start with the first odd integer greater than $8,$ which is $9.$ The next odd integer is $11,$ the one after that is $13,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\n{9,11,13,…}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3135b492-14a6-5ccf-9b83-deecba63f7f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of the Cube Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3adeb09-5140-5fca-9700-c994dedc9c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256595} {:answer/id #uuid "48683ef0-cb9a-5a42-a5dc-8c5f4b4180fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256596} {:answer/id #uuid "8e191c58-93fc-5a21-8269-2e823b43a26e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256597} {:answer/id #uuid "1fab48ab-c6dd-5c37-b4c3-b974a79cd885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256598} {:answer/id #uuid "1a378210-8ecf-5ba8-af29-1ab0d287301c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256599} {:answer/id #uuid "e2129c7c-0469-5c30-af1b-889b4fad5941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600} {:answer/id #uuid "05eb3693-4523-5ff9-ae6d-14f814a75511" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601} {:answer/id #uuid "6564a1f4-1e4f-5275-86da-a58ccba15532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fab48ab-c6dd-5c37-b4c3-b974a79cd885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256598} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2ed87d-8da5-5cf0-924f-c476dada74aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ff78bf-c744-52ed-b02f-687ebfd84d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102961" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. - The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. - $f(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $\nIII. - The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the domain of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false since $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false since $f(-1)=\\sqrt[3]{-1}=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc85958-ad98-5ce2-912c-e2ba70d025ca" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Challenging Expressions Using the Power Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38fc0134-3e32-5936-adb6-119a15f96bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38fc0134-3e32-5936-adb6-119a15f96bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658} :answer-field/id #uuid "b04a25bf-fa89-50dc-84d6-5b32bd7d2347" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "297ab20b-08c4-5fef-9db0-2144be93be03" :question/math-academy-id "q-104652" :question/problem "Given that $\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}}={t}^{n},$ what is $n?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use that $\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}={t}^{-7}$, then we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and finally, we apply the power rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{{t}^{7}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{t}^{-7}} & = \\\\ {({t}^{-7})}^{1/3} & = \\\\ {t}^{(-7)\\cdot (1/3)} & = \\\\ {t}^{-7/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=-\\frac{7}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "851c841f-6e27-5f81-8a0d-0f2439ae3e61" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e9ab2bb-e1b9-5170-9c76-b31d705dee86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256664} {:answer/id #uuid "611e0a96-0804-5d12-9945-f9de947f2ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256665} {:answer/id #uuid "67487606-7811-5761-a83c-868f3a1183f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256666} {:answer/id #uuid "2836c62e-4424-585e-b336-1f4274344dea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256667} {:answer/id #uuid "e3842f0e-4a4c-51b1-8fd8-b5c2cbfcf200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256668} {:answer/id #uuid "b5842364-149e-5b82-ab88-a434e6e3a47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256669} {:answer/id #uuid "42d6f634-f8fc-582e-9020-d11ee4f8e8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256670} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb2485d-327c-5235-8e3e-ceed6d3a2ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb2485d-327c-5235-8e3e-ceed6d3a2ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256671} :answer-field/id #uuid "07029c89-c1ae-54b6-a2f2-8eca277ca242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b16aa53-4832-5f75-8c64-41571c8255c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106090" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f367a4318a1b9d3c2e582f515a2c57058067ed4546f588dc30282f7dd34aac89.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=-1$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\nIII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $-2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $0,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=2,$ and consequently \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\n \nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches the value $y=1,$ and consequently, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n As a result,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "714a3b9b-e0d6-5d88-8c06-907cec122356" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Linear Equation From a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f18187b-0b51-5ce0-872d-df9e45afe42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f18187b-0b51-5ce0-872d-df9e45afe42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2df93ae-3252-5ea5-9051-ef23a01b6c04" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256758}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7eae905-c57c-5087-add9-0cd565ddeaa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-108192" :question/problem "Stephanie is a geologist who began studying land subsidence due to a geological fault. The table below shows the land subsidence  $C,$ in centimeters, $n$ weeks after the study began. How many centimeters of subsidence were there at the beginning of the study?\n\n| $n$ (weeks) | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $C$ (centimeters) | $11$ | $13.5$ | $16$ |\n\nYou may assume that the relationship between $n$ and $C$ is linear.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the number of centimeters increases by $2.5$ every $5$ weeks. In other words, the number of centimeters increases by\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{5}=0.5\n$$\n\ncentimeters per week. Thus, if $A$ is the amount of subsidence at the beginning of the study, then we can model the total subsidence $C$ after $n$ weeks using the equation\n\n$$\nC=A+0.5n.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute one of the pairs of values from the table into this equation and solve for $A.$ After $n=10$ weeks the subsidence was $C=11$ centimeters. We substitute these values into the equation and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =A+0.5n \\\\ 11 & =A+0.5(10) \\\\ 11 & =A+5 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, there were $6$ centimeters of subsidence when the study began."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d190b9-3215-5394-b1d1-61f53b289954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3w+2}{5w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d190b9-3215-5394-b1d1-61f53b289954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3w+2}{5w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256922} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf3fab3-040b-59c3-a6f8-393f329831ac" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256921}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "152bb5fb-6b78-5bf6-87a1-aa5093238c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-113208" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $5w,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2w-5}{5w}+\\frac{w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-5+w+7}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2w+w)+(-5+7)}{5w} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w+2}{5w} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71507084-d077-551e-b813-1960adbe78dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7-10f}{5d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71507084-d077-551e-b813-1960adbe78dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7-10f}{5d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256928} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48fb929-3776-5058-bd67-18d5b2d30c0f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14660835-267f-58f5-b918-aac4bda4695d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113229" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{7}{5d}=g$ and $\\frac{2f}{d}=h,$ what is $g-h?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\ng-h=\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5d$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2f}{d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{5d}-\\frac{10f}{5d} & = \\\\ \\frac{7-10f}{5d} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76fa3985-f3c7-55bf-a7e0-5ef5d389ce4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76fa3985-f3c7-55bf-a7e0-5ef5d389ce4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256931} :answer-field/id #uuid "63fb932b-9122-530a-b4d5-034337355a73" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256930}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e80c14c-c7e8-540f-b03b-760b7afaf9a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113302" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $4v(1+w)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3w}{v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w}{4v(2+w)}-\\frac{5}{4v(2+w)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12w-5}{4v(2+w)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3e4295-1d50-5ed7-adfd-ecb9993c4b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3e4295-1d50-5ed7-adfd-ecb9993c4b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256934} :answer-field/id #uuid "056d9d45-d2b5-5d01-adec-bbca6fd8c112" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256933}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c739dfc-d398-5a70-9f99-96abdc52d307" :question/math-academy-id "q-113308" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $(t-3)(u+4)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $t-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{t-3}{t-3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{u+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{t}{(t-3)(u+4)}+\\frac{5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5(t-3)}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{t+5t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6t-15}{(t-3)(u+4)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4127f-1253-5aee-b524-84e5c6b2bc64" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Angles From Right, Straight, and Full Angles Using Algebra"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5333780-be76-575d-8a64-79b318f11854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5333780-be76-575d-8a64-79b318f11854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257115} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6a97bc-3c12-5f1e-920d-ec4b24a3bd84" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764f27f2-ac09-5d2f-9141-43d1eab813c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114789" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/19d372d897d3bff77348bf0e08fdae344243901f533da6dab6017b7833534045.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that $∠AOB$ and $∠COD$ are right angles. Hence,\n\n$$\nm∠AOB={90}^{∘},\\,m∠COD={90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince the measure of a full angle is ${360}^{∘},$ we can form an equation and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOB+m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOA & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {90}^{∘}+2x+{90}^{∘}+(8x+{30}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {210}^{∘}+10x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6727fdd-a614-5ead-9273-08c0a7fec997" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Linear Combination of Two Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f00ea6a0-13f9-519c-9283-1433fc6754ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00ea6a0-13f9-519c-9283-1433fc6754ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257124} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d71f43-0198-534a-a76e-78a5c7ff66fc" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c3c137-8602-5f1d-96d8-8266ec5a6048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12c3c137-8602-5f1d-96d8-8266ec5a6048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257126} :answer-field/id #uuid "de2d9f41-421d-5340-a0d1-369099542203" :answer-field/key "r1c2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e54d8f1-4f94-5d30-b05f-3a8b2466941f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e54d8f1-4f94-5d30-b05f-3a8b2466941f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257128} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e14cc66-77c0-5c25-ba6e-f662695dd674" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a707a8e1-6dba-5c6c-96e5-30b0f82458c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a707a8e1-6dba-5c6c-96e5-30b0f82458c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257130} :answer-field/id #uuid "440e4888-7883-5d03-99f9-c1a1ee936bb1" :answer-field/key "r2c2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257129}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68388deb-c99d-58dc-919b-11e840c4302b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11532" :question/problem "Calculate $2H-3L$, where\n$H=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$L=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each entry of $H$ by $2$ and each entry of $L$ by $3,$ and then subtract the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2H-3L & =2[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 0 & 3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-0 & -4-3 \\\\ 4-6 & -2-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4a8d54a-c034-5646-b804-0dc96f6c21d0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Radical Function With an Even Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e9f0d5-d775-5b27-b8dd-388be0c6d41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37e9f0d5-d775-5b27-b8dd-388be0c6d41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257148} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b0bafd-d05d-5701-a299-8160e6bdf2c1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257147}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04cac8d8-af4c-5d63-ad0a-aa9d84b0fb91" :question/math-academy-id "q-115818" :question/problem "Find the domain of $m(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[8]{2x+6}+2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The domain of $y=\\sqrt[8]{x}$ is \n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $m(x)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt[8]{2x+6}+2,$ we replace $x$ with $2x+6$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n2x+6\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -6 \\\\ x & \\ge -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=m(x)$ is $x\\in [-3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=m(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ed39939f068dfa0d93b3177deee4c4a822aaaae821911b83153e29eb7a0ccac6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb85b52-cd40-5469-b2f5-ceee2f0bf0cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity From a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ae285e-dde4-5553-9057-3c9ba16bf14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ae285e-dde4-5553-9057-3c9ba16bf14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257178} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e9cc90-4d03-5d55-82e0-dda75d65eb22" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d7893f2-23b8-5b1b-b710-b842231b1ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116814" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2e19b7a47baa207e10614aea3887b943bce87e759e10a204f516ac78b1539569.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $B.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{40}^{∘}+3x+(7x-{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {30}^{∘}+10x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={150}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nm∠B=3x=3({15}^{∘})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cda2e87-1a65-5075-96fd-98a8a4fd7d7a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7c4db9d-25e2-5919-b3ae-fcdeff4ae1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7c4db9d-25e2-5919-b3ae-fcdeff4ae1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257264} :answer-field/id #uuid "70bc7d00-d148-5978-b0f0-bbea8032d6aa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257263}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28fafe7a-73c7-5a73-a89e-2b98b4be1492" :question/math-academy-id "q-118638" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0beb4cb3deb6bf528f399d93f4fad9e9f4ddb141f7187608bdf9a9bd047eb9a7.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{WX}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WZ & =XZ \\\\ 2x+2 & =17-x \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cca7a0b2-c939-52cc-9ca0-0b90e5590375" :knowledge-point/title "The Volume of a Cube: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28958a92-cdfd-563c-bf7b-698f8dd3dfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28958a92-cdfd-563c-bf7b-698f8dd3dfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257267} :answer-field/id #uuid "347549b9-f010-5796-ba6b-0b4c4671ca4f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257266}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a21337e-3e5a-51b3-bf08-4393c6f42c40" :question/math-academy-id "q-118705" :question/problem "An electric oven in the shape of a cube has a side length of $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the oven?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the volume of the oven.\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the side length of the oven.\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd}$ into the formula, we get \n\n$$\nV={(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{yd})}^{3}=\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the oven is $\\frac{8}{27}\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e16dc99-c51a-588c-b1ec-f51a05ceba80" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Particular Side in Relation to One Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989b3bf6-5a07-5ab4-b5d7-dbdc45571ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "989b3bf6-5a07-5ab4-b5d7-dbdc45571ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257441} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f31e72-927e-5575-94be-e193af9d2229" :answer-field/key "adjacent" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854e0318-6469-5b80-b266-bdb9813587bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "854e0318-6469-5b80-b266-bdb9813587bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257443} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4a8e47-9d3c-5924-9054-1e6c41cc81fa" :answer-field/key "hypotenuse" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf89322-8a22-55aa-8e94-7f3138e8edcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bf89322-8a22-55aa-8e94-7f3138e8edcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257445} :answer-field/id #uuid "c20bad86-5a8c-51ea-8330-f45e1fa747e7" :answer-field/key "opposite" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257444}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bda30b07-0b71-5ff3-8e7f-c4c74b56e446" :question/math-academy-id "q-121583" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2b09fee84a9408526d985220a79ede00b38f811f5439ec74bbfa398c542223a7.png)\n\nRelative to $∠TVU,$ what are the lengths of the opposite, adjacent, and hypotenuse, respectively?\n\nOpposite: {{opposite}}\n\nAdjacent: {{adjacent}}\n\nHypotenuse: {{hypotenuse}}" :question/worked-solution "Relative to $∠TVU,$ \n\n- the opposite side is $\\overset{―}{TU},$ and its length is $1,$\n\n- the adjacent side is $\\overset{―}{UV},$ and its length is $2,$ and\n\n- the hypotenuse is $\\overset{―}{TV},$ and its length is $\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c31701-e6cc-5cb1-817d-35a2b1c78231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257661} {:answer/id #uuid "4058f54f-8bd8-5a0d-9f06-c4f3defccdcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257662} {:answer/id #uuid "740ce852-d22c-5e0a-a994-5bd0af7b2ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257663} {:answer/id #uuid "8361e86a-95c6-50c6-a121-fe5c076efb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257664} {:answer/id #uuid "b229ae06-a681-5877-85a9-7ead2a40afdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b229ae06-a681-5877-85a9-7ead2a40afdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257665} :answer-field/id #uuid "d81ce965-92b6-5a22-89ae-97cf7e65a014" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b29e44ae-a5ba-5626-8819-be052caaf1f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124489" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n-4)!$ for any $n\\ge 5?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-4)!=(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-4)! & =(n-4)\\cdot \\overset{(n-5)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-5)\\cdot (n-6)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c9e9a11-582b-5642-abc1-e0e9e2083735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-4)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257668} {:answer/id #uuid "98026112-288f-5503-a229-829433cfd2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257669} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd1f146-2552-52bc-b334-8b349e7466b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257670} {:answer/id #uuid "3c02fb37-5681-57d8-ae4b-1a889a63578f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257671} {:answer/id #uuid "11fbcafb-2e83-5b94-bba5-8d6cc09697ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c02fb37-5681-57d8-ae4b-1a889a63578f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257671} :answer-field/id #uuid "533e9597-0fd9-5bfe-82f6-85bd0bdf80be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e61051d8-ea2b-5702-bf82-3dab92879c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124492" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n-2)!$ for any $n\\ge 4?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n-2)!=(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-2)! & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot \\overset{(n-4)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n-4)\\cdot (n-5)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n-2)\\cdot (n-3)\\cdot (n-4)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1a423d-00ae-5c18-b176-f6564918ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257675} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbfd842-2bd3-5d73-a317-4d7d71c5608d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+1)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257676} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5bf00b-17e6-5bda-9736-dc067b0bef91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257677} {:answer/id #uuid "dd27a54b-d59e-5618-9e13-1b28c5c7a7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257678} {:answer/id #uuid "f8153453-717a-5fa9-bdcd-0cb27d0e8fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a423d-00ae-5c18-b176-f6564918ba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257675} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79e6af0-433a-53d2-9e69-bef7522f0bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257674}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c74243-2d3a-5a86-b262-41a397c01a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124498" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+3)!$ for any $n\\ge -1?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+3)! & =(n+3)\\cdot \\overset{(n+2)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "745c18ba-b4b2-5ca2-b01b-f674d042bf96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257682} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc665a8-0d4b-52ee-8146-081448b0b6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+6)\\cdot (n+4)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257683} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd3b879-c086-5ba1-b450-6fce908c41c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot (n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257684} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b5da1-db41-598c-a0fb-ea377f30778f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257685} {:answer/id #uuid "945bfc0d-31e2-5ee2-b31f-9eda3f097d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "745c18ba-b4b2-5ca2-b01b-f674d042bf96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257682} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e922b76-cdcd-576d-b343-fa35667ad6d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257681}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e762a89-d904-530d-b468-be031bc28729" :question/math-academy-id "q-124502" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+6)!$ for any $n\\ge -3?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+6)! & =(n+6)\\cdot \\overset{(n+5)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91cbf96f-9d5a-5942-afcf-44040736fccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91cbf96f-9d5a-5942-afcf-44040736fccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257804} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38e78ed-df9c-58d1-90b8-7b6526913134" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11d30a39-1dc6-581f-aa48-ba6b816cb779" :question/math-academy-id "q-125550" :question/problem "What is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16ad6bbc-b8c4-52f6-9f24-0d8494d83b94" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Definition of the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "704724ba-430f-55aa-a1a9-5de0b0cb597d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "704724ba-430f-55aa-a1a9-5de0b0cb597d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257810} :answer-field/id #uuid "c172dac0-13f1-5511-a417-f0c192f117e4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257809}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5f988bd-3bbf-5601-9c67-d713b7d993de" :question/math-academy-id "q-126989" :question/problem "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u={x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{{x}^{2}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n/x} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={({e}^{{x}^{2}})}^{1/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ccdde46-a4ab-517e-910b-571a3a166b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in an Expression Involving the Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c6267b-b6e1-588e-a95e-c1c0f10bdcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c6267b-b6e1-588e-a95e-c1c0f10bdcac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257813} :answer-field/id #uuid "71885dd8-5771-5616-abe8-685fda47e1b8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257812}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b0686b1-334e-545d-8566-76f487f19d14" :question/math-academy-id "q-127614" :question/problem "If\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(D)=-32$, what is the value of $x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 6 \\\\ 2x & x-2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x\\cdot (x-2)-6\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-32$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x & =-32 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-16x+32 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e1b4442-eca7-5283-9d5d-5bcbd1d67975" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minor in a 3x3 Matrix With Integer Entries"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedb97a5-a477-5288-a35c-c5df85b5d92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cedb97a5-a477-5288-a35c-c5df85b5d92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257816} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f71b00e-de6c-5483-9764-a413e5bd29b4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257815}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7eee920-abd1-5e92-872a-5f2d162a508d" :question/math-academy-id "q-127701" :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{32}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ (third row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $3$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}8 & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 3 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{32}=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =8\\cdot 6-4\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =48+20 \\\\  & =68.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0d0fdb7-9849-5aca-b494-3b013b2eb035" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Square Root of a Perfect Square Containing a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56097d18-99a7-5c64-b9ca-26271383889b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56097d18-99a7-5c64-b9ca-26271383889b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257819} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e3ae06-b55b-542f-b8fe-0458d29c6d2e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257818}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64bdd06f-725c-53d8-b1bd-371eda2884d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-129471" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${z}^{2},25,$ and ${y}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot y\\cdot 5\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z}{5y}\\cdot \\frac{z}{5y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{z}{5y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5|\\cdot |y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eddc1dd-c230-5bc7-af48-2e6fe476b329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eddc1dd-c230-5bc7-af48-2e6fe476b329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257828} :answer-field/id #uuid "438f7e7f-aba9-5f26-8a88-a8e64341593f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257827}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c33c986-dc79-5316-96bc-a5c56e151eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-130520" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b9c13f97b40699c11e5b3f4f1c77457177cf83418ce08d7a30420117b816f687.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(-1)+f(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-1,$ we have $y=0.$ So, $f(-1)=0.$\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1)+f(3)=0+(-2)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b977c7e-eb1a-5dec-87f2-788ad6289b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b977c7e-eb1a-5dec-87f2-788ad6289b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257831} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7960ed4-5ae5-5275-b08e-12ab6f8ca413" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257830}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13175d1d-ea3b-53e4-8a6a-5d9e08d8c7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-130569" :question/problem "Evaluate $f(2)$, where $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=2$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x+7}+{4}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+7}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}+16 \\\\  & =3+16 \\\\  & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b201f4cd-b39c-5ca5-9565-0618859cd15a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Equations Using Cross Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2845182-faa2-54d5-a2aa-67d66eba400b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{b-12}{b-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2845182-faa2-54d5-a2aa-67d66eba400b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{b-12}{b-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257973} :answer-field/id #uuid "540038d1-4d47-5665-9113-5292e89fafa9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58fae30e-8e99-56e8-af64-b904d4ec79eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133897" :question/problem "If $\\frac{ab-b}{8}=\\frac{a-3}{2},$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $a$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ab-b}{8} & =\\frac{a-3}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (ab-b) & =8\\cdot (a-3) \\\\ 2ab-2b & =8a-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $8a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-2b & =8a-24 \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a & =8a-24-8a \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $2b$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-2b-8a & =-24 \\\\ 2ab-2b-8a+2b & =2b-24 \\\\ 2ab-8a & =2b-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $a$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $a$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $a$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2ab-8a & =2b-24 \\\\ a(2b-8) & =2b-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(2b-8)$ to isolate $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2b-8) & =2b-24 \\\\ \\frac{a\\cancel{(2b-8)}}{\\cancel{2b-8}} & =\\frac{2b-24}{2b-8} \\\\ a & =\\frac{2b-24}{2b-8} \\\\ a & =\\frac{2(b-12)}{2(b-4)} \\\\ a & =\\frac{b-12}{b-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $b-4\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccadedc-ac9d-501f-ac6f-af1d32f00024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257976} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c896c4-9756-5b21-afd7-840dfc0de007" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257977} {:answer/id #uuid "58668d09-0f3a-5dc7-9e04-380e43c43f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257978} {:answer/id #uuid "f10e9224-203c-5056-a43b-1139e16b53e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257979} {:answer/id #uuid "eddc8efc-da25-514e-ae6d-122bd4f49d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257980} {:answer/id #uuid "935540f8-c858-5bef-8d72-cd50516436da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257981} {:answer/id #uuid "5aee3821-2280-5178-9380-34cbe7e976d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257982} {:answer/id #uuid "5dde2dc4-e38c-5c9e-836a-729f2fd9d7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "935540f8-c858-5bef-8d72-cd50516436da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257981} :answer-field/id #uuid "4271acfa-9f19-528d-b7fe-83b00c07c70b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257975}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2ae6b9a-e5a8-56dc-8196-49d72b8c47d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-134130" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/bc3809e55e601738d56be6885475ed442dec1fb3a0d2e19da14be045510f73dc.png)\n\nThe above scatter plot shows the relationship between age and years of experience of a group of professional soccer players. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - Only one player has exactly $10$ years of experience.\nII. - There are two players aged $21.$\nIII. - None of the players has fewer than $6$ years of experience." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose vertical coordinate equals $10,$ namely, $(21,10).$ This means only one of the players has exactly $10$ years of experience.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/209759f5d70fd8587740d1da840f9da838a3c9a055a0d1e962f76c1cd6341fd3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has two dots whose horizontal coordinate equals $21,$ namely, $(21,10)$ and $(21,12).$ This means that two players are $21$ years old.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one player has fewer than $6$ years of experience. The dot below the horizontal line represents this player.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "429a1aa9-f72c-50b7-a22c-27192a8b7efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "429a1aa9-f72c-50b7-a22c-27192a8b7efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257986} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca19158c-3589-51ce-9250-40e2ad38e966" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbd05050-bcf2-5bb4-9011-c5d2c51ceabc" :question/math-academy-id "q-134527" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2286d1b9-fc6e-578c-9ad0-c84396ca4e90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b2cd4eb-1be9-5824-ba37-dac440a9b9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-\\sqrt6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b2cd4eb-1be9-5824-ba37-dac440a9b9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2-\\sqrt6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257989} :answer-field/id #uuid "b67e56ce-7857-5d94-806c-15b87097d958" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4030555-5fa1-5b12-945b-819c15276e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4030555-5fa1-5b12-945b-819c15276e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2+\\sqrt6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c861b29c-8354-57c8-957d-c7216336afc5" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5b37049-7913-52ac-bd10-29e98dfac38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13454" :question/problem "Find the $x$-intercepts of $y=-{x}^{2}-4x+2.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the points on the parabola where $y=0.$ So, we have the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's multiply by $-1$ to make the leading coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-{x}^{2}-4x+2) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is difficult to factor, but we can solve it using the quadratic formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-(4)(1)(-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{24}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{4\\cdot 6}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $x=-2-\\sqrt{6}$ and $x=-2+\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a31c2b6-585a-5715-98f8-4b82605f4c22" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Containing Cube Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66eebd56-226b-55bb-adcb-d0c87f674a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66eebd56-226b-55bb-adcb-d0c87f674a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257994} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e6a0d8-bcf5-503d-b979-266bd990300a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257993}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e38c546d-9295-59dc-a5d8-1c4e46ac7fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-134565" :question/problem "Simplify $2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the product rule for radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{40} & =\\sqrt[3]{8\\cdot 5}=\\sqrt[3]{8}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{5}=2\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7\\sqrt[3]{40} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+7(2\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt[3]{5}+2\\sqrt{6}+14\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2\\sqrt[3]{5}+14\\sqrt[3]{5}) & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+(2+14)\\sqrt[3]{5} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6}+16\\sqrt[3]{5} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa97e57a-70d8-5708-8b7d-fa05604a3f88" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Residual Plots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1198c783-6d09-59c8-83de-ea02f8aaafbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} {:answer/id #uuid "597a71cf-9864-5336-b079-cb365d18d538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258005} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb50253-a975-5059-90ce-f2edaee71701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258006} {:answer/id #uuid "60f285b6-518e-544a-83b3-61a07eb08a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258007} {:answer/id #uuid "2d51af7a-49e9-5c4e-b237-6a5f7b502cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258008} {:answer/id #uuid "0909f57e-a765-5887-be02-0912931e4062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258009} {:answer/id #uuid "a50c314e-f8f7-5634-8543-bfcb643fd0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258010} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c32971-4119-5de3-be91-98e96faa25f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f285b6-518e-544a-83b3-61a07eb08a67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258007} :answer-field/id #uuid "f244f4e1-13b7-5e68-bfa8-3655a8868455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5d30017-4bac-5ef9-907f-2a33c3ed66b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134815" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/cec25c6ab182494771824bae8c2182bf38b57e2d1e81d913e3e49916c8cc3d60.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - When $x=8,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\nII. - The residuals appear to be randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\nIII. - This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=8$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates  this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e62b0e-adda-5d35-aeb9-961057dce8f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum or Difference of Three or More Matrices Using Distributivity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fea0f1-db68-53b8-b64d-2d81bb965cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19fea0f1-db68-53b8-b64d-2d81bb965cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258014} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8e1263-f3fe-579d-8e20-d7beb1ea8ae8" :answer-field/key "r1c1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87bc8ad6-e689-5792-9a80-b23182d9d445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87bc8ad6-e689-5792-9a80-b23182d9d445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258016} :answer-field/id #uuid "db11b31c-592b-587b-af27-7145a6680bc1" :answer-field/key "r1c2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e2da5a-b65e-5688-8adf-7728bf6c3d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25e2da5a-b65e-5688-8adf-7728bf6c3d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258018} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a458555-43c2-595b-9bea-97070415fc6b" :answer-field/key "r2c1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258017} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827ee7ec-62cb-5317-8a23-cb58c8957e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827ee7ec-62cb-5317-8a23-cb58c8957e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258020} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f5c65a-f17f-5663-b1ae-b6481c8333c5" :answer-field/key "r2c2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb48a317-e064-5f22-b418-b65055f852ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-13550" :question/problem "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow 1, column 1: {{r1c1}}\n\nRow 1, column 2: {{r1c2}}\n\nRow 2, column 1: {{r2c1}}\n\nRow 2, column 2: {{r2c2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0141126d-4951-54c1-9360-677112b53c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0141126d-4951-54c1-9360-677112b53c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258026} :answer-field/id #uuid "79684820-2beb-5b18-be3e-c78099f2c767" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54cf059d-83d7-54df-aac4-8e7a8a49e274" :question/math-academy-id "q-13586" :question/problem "If $f(x)=3x+8$ and $g(x)=-5x+7,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(3x+8)-(-5x+7) \\\\  & =(3x-(-5x))+(8-7) \\\\  & =8x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df2d8f5-89b3-57e4-8f4a-d270b42092e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df2d8f5-89b3-57e4-8f4a-d270b42092e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258029} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e585b38-6c42-5ef4-ad3e-c2eee41d3fb6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258028}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "084cf399-5cc4-5ae8-9e6f-6bbf5eb1b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-13594" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=7x+3$ and $g(x)=2-5x,$ find $(fg)(1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(1),$ we find $f(1)$ and $g(1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(1)$ and $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =7(1)+3=10 \\\\ g(1) & =2-5(1)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(1)=f(1)g(1)=10\\cdot (-3)=-30\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(7x+3)(2-5x) \\\\  & =-35{x}^{2}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(1) & =-35(1)^{2}-(1)+6 \\\\  & =-35-1+6 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50417128-ff7a-5375-8f9b-6380919d7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50417128-ff7a-5375-8f9b-6380919d7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032} :answer-field/id #uuid "60051f1b-1cc5-57b3-9d12-2baaf0da0965" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258031}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c2c26f6-ca4a-50fd-a1f5-f21eba02c0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-13595" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x+2$ and $g(x)=x-5$, find $(fg)(-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)+2=1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)-5=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-1)=f(-1)g(-1)=1\\cdot (-6)=-6\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(x+2)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-1) & =(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-10 \\\\  & =1+3-10 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be6ccc92-c990-5058-ada1-3e61078deb9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be6ccc92-c990-5058-ada1-3e61078deb9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258315} :answer-field/id #uuid "aec5bcbf-664d-5504-9bb2-d2b854602e61" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258314}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d86f908-7737-5796-8d80-bd80a4b73f99" :question/math-academy-id "q-14365" :question/problem "If the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(7,-4)$ and $(a,b)$ is $(2,1),$\nthen $a+b=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The given points are ${{(7,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})},$ ${{(a,b)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(2,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}\\,.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\ (2,1) & =(\\frac{7+a}{2},\\frac{-4+b}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2=\\frac{7+a}{2} \\\\ 1=\\frac{-4+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,{\\begin{aligned}a=-3 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $(a,b)=(-3,6)$ and $a+b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "469f0c76-3641-5a56-82bb-9ac807ff2017" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Volume With the Correct Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212eb15e-a3d4-5572-9f85-da6667c67885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,000\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "212eb15e-a3d4-5572-9f85-da6667c67885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125\\,000\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258321} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b7b85e-3e22-57e0-bc6a-a83dfb2f8f57" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a41776af-70f9-5b80-bd56-808b6173a158" :question/math-academy-id "q-144195" :question/problem "A cube has a side length of $50\\,\\text{yd}.$ What is the volume of the cube?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the volume $V$ of a cube, we cube its side length. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(50\\,\\text{yd})^{3} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{yd}\\times 50\\,\\text{yd}\\times 50\\,\\text{yd} \\\\  & =(50\\times 50\\times 50)\\times (\\text{yd}\\times \\text{yd}\\times \\text{yd}) \\\\  & =125\\,000\\,{\\text{yd}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9903e779-9ba3-5920-8450-53ab46f9cc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9903e779-9ba3-5920-8450-53ab46f9cc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258835} :answer-field/id #uuid "505379a5-d388-5642-8c53-555ca99c500e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aaff4c14-16ea-5dec-8f92-9c9a5e0030ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-20276" :question/problem "Frank has a bag with $10$ different balls. In how many ways can Frank select a set of $8$ balls from the bag, considering that the order does not matter?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ balls, so $n=10.$ We want to select $8$ of those balls from the bag, so we have $r=8.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential sets of $8$ balls is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{8} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-8)!\\,8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{2!8!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot \\cancel{8!}}{2!\\cancel{8!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{2} \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c880ac-41a9-543f-b7a1-b4dfcc5de99d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Fractions in Both Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38d448d8-75d3-5040-a188-af3eb10d7682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38d448d8-75d3-5040-a188-af3eb10d7682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258885} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7df017-81ad-5529-9346-295e76e57b85" :answer-field/key "a" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfaad01c-ff25-516e-9912-c42b36d3564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfaad01c-ff25-516e-9912-c42b36d3564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258887} :answer-field/id #uuid "25bdc574-1350-5311-8164-7343ef90bab7" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258886}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50eece3f-06ca-554e-b56c-6eb5ad4a01ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-216064" :question/problem "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$a$: {{a}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $15,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}a-\\frac{1}{2}b=1 & |\\times 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}a+\\frac{1}{3}b=3 & |\\times 15\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3,$ then we will get a $-3a$ term that can cancel with the $3a$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a-2b=4 & |\\,\\times (-3) \\\\ 3a+5b=45 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3a+6b=-12 \\\\ 3a+5b=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{-3a}+6b & =-12 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}+5b & =45 \\\\ (6b+5b) & =(-12+45) \\\\ 11b & =33 \\\\ b & =\\frac{33}{11} \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $b=3$ into the first equation to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-2b & =4 \\\\ a-2(3) & =4 \\\\ a-6 & =4 \\\\ a & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $a=10,\\,b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a16e0d4b-9508-5498-a5da-b9ddbe86df26" :knowledge-point/title "Converting From Radians to Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da32ee4a-368f-5bda-b236-bd7fa0542f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da32ee4a-368f-5bda-b236-bd7fa0542f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258945} :answer-field/id #uuid "588fd770-7077-5f8c-b04c-1aba35df695e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9450f9ea-a825-529d-9a2f-f0f06bdb892c" :question/math-academy-id "q-22963" :question/problem "What is the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ radians in degrees?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\cdot \\pi }{9\\cdot \\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {20}^{∘}\\cdot \\pi }{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{100}^{∘}\\cancel{\\pi }}{\\cancel{\\pi }} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to ${100}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c856a2-1456-55d7-b5b2-a5c22576cfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c856a2-1456-55d7-b5b2-a5c22576cfc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259021} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad4a00f5-1b51-5614-8cb1-f90d49d0fe81" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259020}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d5a3ff6-af15-5e95-830a-b355da2ec009" :question/math-academy-id "q-24882" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t)$ at $t=10.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10t) & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd738be-308a-54b8-aa4c-7acd6d60b691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inequality Corresponding to a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4ef1f9e-eecd-5ab7-85a9-5d79005f8346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4ef1f9e-eecd-5ab7-85a9-5d79005f8346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259332} :answer-field/id #uuid "64ea8576-917c-5ae9-bfb2-b3b51b23432c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3410dcfc-f781-556b-b8a2-eb3039fbcda3" :question/math-academy-id "q-29440" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/51a05d6b2fe04eb825b8048281b6d1e94b65df9f6f80c6e8965f9e414c5b360c.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,1)$ and $(1,-1)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-1}{1-0}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=-2x+1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e7e1deb-efdb-5549-8e83-ffcba093d481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Prime Factorization of a Three-Digit Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8584c7f4-aee0-51c5-8848-9809d4b3fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8584c7f4-aee0-51c5-8848-9809d4b3fba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260385} :answer-field/id #uuid "b83c8ac8-1b0a-5849-87e0-b94e6262669e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260384}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "975f0bb3-e993-5f9b-a3ec-3b810e3cbf9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43632" :question/problem "What is the prime decomposition of  $126?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $126$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $126$ is $2,$ and $126=2\\cdot 63.$ We write these factors below $126$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b4f520ebadd00c650f5172e14943f67e67bc63f8e38a1ecd3fe86a7e7ab74ad4.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $2$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $63$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $63$ is $3,$ and $63=3\\cdot 21.$ We write these factors below $63$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following: \n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is\n\n$$\n126=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e7e1deb-efdb-5549-8e83-ffcba093d481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Prime Factorization of a Three-Digit Integer"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21381e4c-dbdb-5dbf-9740-7260353e0ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21381e4c-dbdb-5dbf-9740-7260353e0ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260388} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b3c1fb-965d-5446-b945-9970eb8d409a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260387}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e736a28-8e89-585c-8f2b-ce912c35bf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-43634" :question/problem "What is the prime decomposition of $315?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the prime factorization of a number, we can use a factor tree.\n\nFirst, we need to express $315$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $315$ is $3,$ and $315=3\\cdot 105.$ We write these factors below $315$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/35f5150e72285c0c706af351aa9827bf7010e22fa0dd49a07569466006c2bff1.png)\n\nWe draw a circle around $3$ because it's prime. \n\nNext, we need to express $105$ as the product of its *smallest* prime factor and *another* factor. \n\nThe *smallest* prime factor of $105$ is $3,$ and $105=3\\cdot 35.$ We write these factors below $105$ on our tree with the smallest prime factor on the left-hand side.\n\nWe carry on like this, stopping only when all of the endpoints are prime. By doing this, we get the following:\n\nSo finally, our prime factorization is \n\n$$\n315=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 5\\cdot 7={3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af38f6e8-af5e-5964-954b-b721b05c6380" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Quadratic Equation in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62c993ae-3b7f-54a9-8915-64cf4748902a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^2-3x+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c993ae-3b7f-54a9-8915-64cf4748902a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^2-3x+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260391} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fecff78-4823-57f7-ba95-93f3f42f0f86" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260390}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d8626cf-33ce-5080-be62-864632b78a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-44045" :question/problem "Write the quadratic equation $(2x-1)(x-1)=-2{x}^{2}$ in standard form.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are constants and $a\\ne 0.$\n\nTo write this equation in standard form, we start by expanding the binomial product on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-1)(x-1) & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x(x-1)-1(x-1) & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-x+1 & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect the ${x}^{2}$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x+1+2{x}^{2} & =-2{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2})-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that our equation is in the standard form of a quadratic equation with $a=4,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=1.$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a quadratic equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59230000-ef0e-56c0-ab06-17676d817d5a" :knowledge-point/title "Cross-Multiplying with a Negative Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e802ab2-21bf-5bbc-9750-1cc6f48bf884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e802ab2-21bf-5bbc-9750-1cc6f48bf884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260394} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a52611a-e49a-575e-a27a-b6d409261943" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fcdcd9f3-7122-5f59-93b6-53cc1a94a313" :question/math-academy-id "q-44205" :question/problem "Solve for $t$ where $\\frac{1-3t}{5}=-\\frac{4+t}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We transfer the negative into the numerator of the fraction, and proceed with cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-3t}{5} & =-\\frac{4+t}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-(4+t)}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-3t}{5} & =\\frac{-4-t}{2} \\\\ (1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)\\cdot 2 & =5\\cdot (-4-t) \\\\ 2-6t & =-20-5t \\\\ 2-6t+5t & =-20-5t+5t \\\\ 2-t & =-20 \\\\ 2-t-2 & =-20-2 \\\\ -t & =-22 \\\\ -1\\cdot t & =-22 \\\\ \\frac{-1\\cdot t}{-1} & =\\frac{-22}{-1} \\\\ t & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line Given a Point and a Slope"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4ee81a-ccfb-5af4-9d7f-6b289d2cd64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c4ee81a-ccfb-5af4-9d7f-6b289d2cd64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260397} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5b7cc0-e4c3-528c-aa88-a10de0acf241" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea076972-7904-5cb7-87e8-ab329a78a15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea076972-7904-5cb7-87e8-ab329a78a15c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260399} :answer-field/id #uuid "59769b53-693c-54d6-bb7a-05801ddb3206" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4e56e6-52fc-51ba-afd3-0b7b7237eab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-44298" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $y=15x+5.$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "At the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15x+5 \\\\ 0 & =15x+5 \\\\ -15x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{-15x}{(-15)} & =\\frac{5}{(-15)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-\\frac{1}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "821bc74d-363a-5f3d-bca0-3359ec5ce7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "821bc74d-363a-5f3d-bca0-3359ec5ce7dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260405} :answer-field/id #uuid "6851ed09-f669-5650-858d-0ed4cd6c1a02" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260404}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40918e56-d78a-554c-b936-9df96a583818" :question/math-academy-id "q-44571" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-3{x}^{2}+12x=-k$ has two distinct real solutions.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is positive.\n\nFirst, we write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12x+k=0.\n$$\n\nWe have $a=-3,$ $b=12,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={12}^{2}-4(-3)(k) \\\\  & =144+12k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & >0 \\\\ 144+12k & >0 \\\\ 12k & >-144 \\\\ k & >-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have two distinct real solutions if $k>-12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60270e0c-a60c-5a5f-8993-3d8362081c8f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Common Logarithms Using a Calculator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb38578-339f-547d-a018-7c880ceec33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.408" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbb38578-339f-547d-a018-7c880ceec33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.408" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260472} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90c1f9c-f963-5c96-a354-804058c647dc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10395e19-686b-58f5-b08a-69774a480eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-47274" :question/problem "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{log}(x)-4\\operatorname{log}(2x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we replace $x=4$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $3$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $6$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =2\\operatorname{log}(4)-4\\operatorname{log}(8) \\\\  & \\approx 2(0.602\\,060)-4(0.903\\,090) \\\\  & =1.204\\,120-3.612\\,360 \\\\  & =-2.408\\,240 \\\\  & \\approx -2.408\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3329cd2b-b1f7-59ca-9a4e-cbb30aadbbf3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Nonlinear Binomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222b1f0d-bbab-5319-bc68-6839d10d8071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81{u}^{6}-16{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222b1f0d-bbab-5319-bc68-6839d10d8071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81{u}^{6}-16{v}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260684} :answer-field/id #uuid "b43b8d1d-204d-5b69-ae25-e71475a20538" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260683}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b141e260-989c-53cf-98aa-d200f8672d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5132" :question/problem "$(9{u}^{3}-4{v}^{2})(9{u}^{3}+4{v}^{2})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b)={a}^{2}-{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=9{u}^{3}$ and $b=4{v}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{u}^{3}-4{v}^{2})(9{u}^{3}+4{v}^{2}) & = \\\\ {(9{u}^{3})}^{2}-{(4{v}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ 81{u}^{6}-16{v}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab16b9c5-9977-585e-b838-599705aeba9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab16b9c5-9977-585e-b838-599705aeba9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260693} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f591d8-00b2-566c-99df-1c6a9f783203" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64793999-0fa3-539c-b945-76159b1213b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-5145" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $2$ is a root of $f(x)=12{x}^{2}-3px.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=2$ is a root, we have $f(2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(2)^{2}-3p(2) & =0 \\\\ 12(4)-6p & =0 \\\\ 48-6p & =0 \\\\ -6p & =-48 \\\\ p & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c5075a-a4cf-5e56-8a6f-2e1636b2ee0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inequality Corresponding to a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da948e5-8e68-529a-910f-d75505610a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge \\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da948e5-8e68-529a-910f-d75505610a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge \\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260792} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d14d67f-4f11-54bb-a59c-80951a1501bf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260791}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c65e4d48-c1de-5aac-888f-ff1772c474c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-6054" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/15f17827141a0a31a7b748ab93c6811afcebd8c66781642554814f126aab8add.png)\n\nWhat is the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=-1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,-1)$ and $(2,0)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{0-(-1)}{2-0}=\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{x}{2}-1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal to\" inequality. Since the shaded region is *above* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge \\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c5075a-a4cf-5e56-8a6f-2e1636b2ee0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inequality Corresponding to a Given Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdad7ef-9f9b-5099-afbb-630b72506a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le \\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fdad7ef-9f9b-5099-afbb-630b72506a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le \\frac{3x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a89d57-9ac5-568c-b8ed-25d62bfdbb01" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260794}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "458f851b-8b42-5a2e-8f64-02fe7feb2c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6056" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/687a951c9cb6e0aaedb444d2ceaede6cb23e8d78198ec66d9dddbf3631dfcd52.png)\n\nWhat is the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,0)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,3)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{3-0}{2-0}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{3x}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal to\" inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{3x}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9275b7b-fb67-5c75-a88f-8d4a402bc14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9275b7b-fb67-5c75-a88f-8d4a402bc14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260801} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a714b0b-b324-5520-b12a-af0a549590d9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a392e0a-6df0-5552-9026-7e13f4379af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-62235" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e6597958db4f674e475a4795e51e3367b775807e67342aa69c7c3d31506ee58c.png)\n\nKate wants to paint a roof that has the form of a square pyramid, as shown above. Find the total surface area that she needs to paint if all four sides have the same surface area. Note that Kate does *not* need to paint the base.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b06219065dd4f22d12056a759d7d10f0a0f44a096f5808e37fb71ff081ab4471.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nEach side face of our solid is a triangle, where the base is $5\\,\\text{m}$ and the height is $8\\,\\text{m}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times 5\\times 8=20\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, the total area of the side surfaces consists of $4$ triangular faces. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area of the sides} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =4\\times 20 \\\\  & =80\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, Kate has to paint $80\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the roof."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6365d04f-b261-5fe5-8a1d-89e22df8935f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6365d04f-b261-5fe5-8a1d-89e22df8935f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260804} :answer-field/id #uuid "0286acbb-2f4d-5e1c-b288-8132393d69cd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a88baf4a-b3d0-5e0e-af18-5660583efd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-62257" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/7e357b1ade83f266d357c41d754a381ce9cdfd9a5fdc9e16ba773af220df198a.png)\n\nAssuming that all side faces have the same height of $6\\,\\text{ft}$, find the total surface area of the square pyramid above using its net.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The net of our pyramid is the following.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the base of the pyramid is a square with a side equal to $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square}=4\\times 4=16\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEach side face of our solid is triangle, where the base is $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and the height is $6\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of the triangle}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times 4\\times 6=12\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =(\\text{Area of the square})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the triangle}) \\\\  & =16+4\\times 48 \\\\  & =16+48 \\\\  & =64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a4fc142-f8be-5f31-882a-e83b75f5f1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260807} {:answer/id #uuid "b73b0fee-d32e-56aa-ad42-83d1a7c3fa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}+5k+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260808} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd33fda-aef8-5fb6-b1e9-74d1da876554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}-3k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260809} {:answer/id #uuid "3c092cc7-e57c-5648-9b3e-ba3be80ea005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}+2k+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260810} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd55d92-a16d-5694-9a50-729422e0bc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73b0fee-d32e-56aa-ad42-83d1a7c3fa14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}+5k+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260808} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54fd3d2-3af0-5e41-8978-da67e08d4bbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260806}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1390df05-6bca-5037-aa71-abe139ff704c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6397" :question/problem "Given that $k\\ge 0$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(k+3)!}{(k+1)k!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(k+3)!=(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ and $(k+1)$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+3)!}{(k+1)k!}= & \\frac{(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k!}{(k+1)\\cdot k!} \\\\ = & \\frac{(k+3)\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)}\\cdot \\cancel{k!}}{\\cancel{(k+1)}\\cdot \\cancel{k!}} \\\\ = & (k+3)(k+2) \\\\ = & {k}^{2}+5k+6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90028ddc-fde1-5c0c-8653-8e93f56c4057" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Square Root of a Negative Number With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "845ca887-5584-5cbd-9e18-81eeca2eb160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845ca887-5584-5cbd-9e18-81eeca2eb160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260907} :answer-field/id #uuid "33511e51-3352-536b-90a3-01c8208e3875" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "829b1669-6318-553d-a1a9-9f8d01e8e9e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66225" :question/problem "$-\\sqrt{-12}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The imaginary unit $\\text{i}$ is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{i}=\\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\nSince $12=4\\cdot 3,$ the given number can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{-12} & =-\\sqrt{12\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7629391d-2609-5c56-8323-73c227842648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7629391d-2609-5c56-8323-73c227842648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260933} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28444a1-1bf8-5433-8bf8-e1265d606a0e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260932}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f3adebb-be1f-5f32-94a7-24221953a6e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66420" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-4$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ find  $(fg)(-2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means  the product of the functions  $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(fg)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then multiply the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4(-2)^{2}-4=12 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply the results:\n\n$$\n(fg)(-2)=f(-2)g(-2)=12\\cdot 6=72\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(fg)(x)$ by multiplying the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(x) & =(4{x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we evaluate $(fg)(-2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(fg)(-2) & =4(-2)^{4}+4(-2)^{2}-8 \\\\  & =64+16-8 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9267840-5ce8-574d-a2fc-24a332eabb5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9267840-5ce8-574d-a2fc-24a332eabb5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260936} :answer-field/id #uuid "61952005-9460-5d76-b16f-7f533c1dbb6b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be22410d-413d-5639-ac9e-a2e4fceb3697" :question/math-academy-id "q-66425" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ and $g(x)=-2x+2,$ find  $(f/g)(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(3),$ we find $f(3)$ and $g(3),$ and then divide the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(3)$ and $g(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =2(3)^{2}-6=12 \\\\ g(3) & =-2(3)+2=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(3)=\\frac{f(3)}{g(3)}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{-2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(3):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f/g)(3) & =\\frac{2(3)^{2}-6}{-2(3)+2}=\\frac{12}{-4}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "972fdea6-91a0-5c04-915e-c97d847aea79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "972fdea6-91a0-5c04-915e-c97d847aea79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942} :answer-field/id #uuid "db91a2a4-f747-5abc-b3f2-624d8c8adf48" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "935c56eb-56da-5d6e-947a-de0e34bd7c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-66481" :question/problem "Find  $(f/g)(-2)$  for $f(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+2x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf the value is undefined, enter \"Undefined\"." :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-2),$ we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-2)$ and $g(-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+(-2)=2 \\\\ g(-2) & =(-2)^{2}+2(-2)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-2)$ by $g(-2).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-2)$  is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a81e85da-c4ce-5353-9a7b-d18daa4caf7d" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Common Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c18914-f970-503f-9a77-1028bab8d491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log(\\frac1{100})=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c18914-f970-503f-9a77-1028bab8d491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log(\\frac1{100})=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260948} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f1ffe92-7dfd-5bed-b30d-4bf73054fd9b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260947}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d9381b1-5d42-59c3-a9db-06ef4e432e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-66543" :question/problem "${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ can be expressed as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=\\frac{1}{100}$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{100})=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b514d4ef-68ad-5fd4-b802-e61ca86aec2c" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a31307d4-fe95-5905-a002-d3737b2b6099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_8(2)=\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31307d4-fe95-5905-a002-d3737b2b6099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_8(2)=\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260951} :answer-field/id #uuid "34785903-564a-59db-bbad-262350972f05" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db59994b-3786-5ceb-b867-dea36ed5e45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-66562" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{8}=2$ can be expressed as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{8}^{1/3}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${8}^{1/3}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=8$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2$ can be expressed as ${log}_{8}2=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a81e85da-c4ce-5353-9a7b-d18daa4caf7d" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Exponential and Logarithmic Form With Common Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844b1b1f-1133-5919-81f9-e8fa8d4433c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log(100000)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844b1b1f-1133-5919-81f9-e8fa8d4433c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log(100000)=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260954} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b33d7f-14b9-5ed8-8917-15d3b8646c25" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260953}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60a81016-2737-5fbd-a942-918bcffa80d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66608" :question/problem "${10}^{5}=100\\,000$ is equivalent to\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{5}=100\\,000$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=100\\,000$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}100\\,000=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475a2c31-42f1-50cb-a261-1edd8acd5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "475a2c31-42f1-50cb-a261-1edd8acd5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260957} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd6482b-f428-5998-8399-25cb88d42795" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260956}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeafaca2-0567-55ba-95dd-d0ca684cdc15" :question/math-academy-id "q-66631" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\sqrt[4]{10}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[4]{10}={10}^{1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\sqrt[4]{10})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{1/4})=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da4785a-0124-517b-9dba-ba012853b83a" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Logarithms With Coefficients Using the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6130991a-8ab3-5716-b016-0c243c85f322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6130991a-8ab3-5716-b016-0c243c85f322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260960} :answer-field/id #uuid "51107b99-a0e6-5ec3-9377-1d83c4b7a6fa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260959}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77269f1f-6854-5aa0-b092-d17b19bb30c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-66772" :question/problem "$6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}(a)-{log}_{n}(b)={log}_{n}(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}(100)-6{log}_{5}(4) & =6({log}_{5}(100)-{log}_{5}(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(\\frac{100}{4}) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}(25)={log}_{5}({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Negative Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9372d3e7-ae97-5646-baa7-7ac94268ee26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9372d3e7-ae97-5646-baa7-7ac94268ee26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260989} :answer-field/id #uuid "21024aa7-8209-5db8-b265-c658bc298b37" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "576be5ad-8307-5956-9086-af2a05212a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-66874" :question/problem "${\\text{i}}^{-3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{{\\text{i}}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $\\text{i},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{\\text{i}}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{{\\text{i}}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{i}}{\\text{i}}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{4}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{4}}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4d4730e-784d-5184-9134-019f0cdedc08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4d4730e-784d-5184-9134-019f0cdedc08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261024} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f33194a-533e-5d5f-aeea-98fb2111edad" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261023}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dedda1be-748d-5578-a854-6406afc3ca44" :question/math-academy-id "q-67160" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=2$, the $6$th term ${a}_{6}=22$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=288.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{6}-{a}_{1}}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{22-2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=2$, $d=4$, and ${S}_{N}=288$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4+4N-4) \\\\ 288 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\ 288 & =2{N}^{2} \\\\ 144 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e475613-ff86-5655-a966-121d622b0bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Interior Angles of a Regular Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee3f5f28-e01b-5a22-a55d-d03e9a923730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee3f5f28-e01b-5a22-a55d-d03e9a923730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261090} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb23736f-adab-5c1d-a381-de6e88c51841" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261089}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3f596ab-ac68-5c7f-b786-c059e7723c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-67891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e17bf0ffc492fc1ed22d97e422cf9926bfac01ac765614bbe63b887853d65e2e.png)\n\nDetermine the measure of each interior angle of a regular octagon.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A regular octagon is an $8$-sided polygon where all the interior angles have the same measure.\n\nThe sum of the measures of its interior angles is \n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\times (8-2)=1\\,{080}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the measure of each interior angle is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,{080}^{∘}}{8}={135}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd2dc211-25cb-5975-a665-f1f6ffd52488" :knowledge-point/title "Combining Various Product and Quotient Rules of Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b55f04-7cd3-5516-801f-54d823312188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b55f04-7cd3-5516-801f-54d823312188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261099} :answer-field/id #uuid "920bc779-3731-5cf3-8bd3-d6fec7f0b5ea" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f339d72-643b-5603-8c4c-84752670bfae" :question/math-academy-id "q-68957" :question/problem "${(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}={6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3-8}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08d7f70-e837-52b5-a04f-b94a8a4abbb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08d7f70-e837-52b5-a04f-b94a8a4abbb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261111} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff98420d-303a-5998-a7ab-08711c468a4e" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261110}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8e64f3e-acff-51b4-ab2e-e9c313440acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-6979" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}}.$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn this case, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{3}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x}{1-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(3+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{3+0+0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02438a3-10ca-5fe5-aad7-ec2c728223e6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring Using Common Factors and Quadratic Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2732344-a0f6-52bd-a5da-e8f1133a71b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}(2x-1)(2x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2732344-a0f6-52bd-a5da-e8f1133a71b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}(2x-1)(2x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261124} :answer-field/id #uuid "176689f2-69ef-5e45-9f54-dfa2d3462bc2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261123}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7f344ca5-bedc-5439-977a-da1c0e3e41a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-69836" :question/problem "Factor the expression $8{x}^{5}-16{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $2{x}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2{x}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{5}-16{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3} & =2{x}^{3}(4{x}^{2}-8x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $4{x}^{2}-8x+3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}(4{x}^{2}-8x+3) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}((4{x}^{2}-6x)+(-2x+3)) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}(2x(2x-3)-1(2x-3)) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}(2x-1)(2x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e8b25b3-5cc4-51b7-8ff9-fc1773797ad4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af7aef1-c83a-5f90-b617-ed5f9a36c70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af7aef1-c83a-5f90-b617-ed5f9a36c70b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261139} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1f79e3-52c1-5680-8033-9d1772967ff2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261138}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08ea8cf2-f1c0-54d8-b1f4-c3ee612c64fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-70042" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x+3$ and $g(x)=-2x+1,$ find $(f+g)(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f+g)(2),$ we find $f(2)$ and $g(2),$ and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(2)$ and $g(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ g(2) & =-2(2)+1=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n(f+g)(2)=f(2)+g(2)=5+(-3)=2\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(x+3)+(-2x+1) \\\\  & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f+g)(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(2) & =-2+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75725a2d-2a8d-563f-99f1-8d071c2ff0c9" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions With Parentheses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b1c28b-4cd8-5ae3-ae71-00d05daa2f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35b1c28b-4cd8-5ae3-ae71-00d05daa2f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261148} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc207bd0-5db3-5bf4-999b-8a51dc1a7a3b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261147}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "275243bf-578c-5ea8-b1eb-e4e03906be2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-70116" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x}$ for $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $2$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-3)}{4-x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2-3)}{4-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(-1)}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}(-1)}{\\cancel{2}} & = \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80d72276-63b0-52c6-b7cf-0f4e2e0392aa" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions With a Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e7f8c8-6042-5f54-ac15-b0fabd4ec7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x-1}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e7f8c8-6042-5f54-ac15-b0fabd4ec7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x-1}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261151} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46904f7-8352-5966-aaa4-c533851a556d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8467b02b-5d78-5734-bca3-a89323c30d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-70142" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{3x+2}{3x}+\\frac{x-3}{3x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We have a common denominator of $3x,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+2}{3x}+\\frac{x-3}{3x} & = \\\\ \\frac{3x+2+x-3}{3x} & = \\\\ \\frac{(3x+x)+(2-3)}{3x} & = \\\\ \\frac{4x-1}{3x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb2231aa-8afa-57df-a43c-cb3add25b2cc" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in Multiple Variables With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0553b981-1930-577d-af1c-21ca7cdd423d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(w+1)(2z+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0553b981-1930-577d-af1c-21ca7cdd423d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(w+1)(2z+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261160} :answer-field/id #uuid "38889a91-063e-55bc-8927-2942a08bfd51" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261159}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10adf2d3-95ec-5701-9e89-61b039914163" :question/math-academy-id "q-70326" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{2}{w+1}-\\frac{4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $(w+1)(2z+3)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $2z+3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w+1}-\\frac{4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2z+3}{2z+3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{w+1}-\\frac{4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2z+3)}{(w+1)(2z+3)}-\\frac{4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2z+3)-4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4z+6-4z}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{(w+1)(2z+3)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7ac4fea-92e3-5f1c-ad24-c9ed600b04ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ac4fea-92e3-5f1c-ad24-c9ed600b04ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261169} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e53765c-9fa0-5a90-9326-4b3f7d537e32" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261168}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5978d3f8-08b2-5532-be4c-579c055ba69b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70358" :question/problem "$\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3t(t+2),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $(t+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{t+1}{t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{t+2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)}{3t(t+2)}-\\frac{t+2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(t+1)-(t+2)}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3t+3-t-2}{3t(t+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2t+1}{3t(t+2)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6489295-a743-5a1b-ba1c-3a1a21887946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6489295-a743-5a1b-ba1c-3a1a21887946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261394} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2fb2ba-1a63-50be-8a2f-06d6293b98a4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e3fd00d-e0e6-58dd-a905-2ad7d3579ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72548" :question/problem "The lines $2y+10x=3$ and $y=px-3$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The second line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=px-3$ is $p.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $2y+10x=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+10x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-5x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $2y+10x=3$ is $-5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot p=-1\\,⟹\\,p=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97c84c3-c608-56b1-bf04-384879e31964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c97c84c3-c608-56b1-bf04-384879e31964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261397} :answer-field/id #uuid "6392d35b-90a6-526e-b49f-ded054099625" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8714b293-e1b5-58d0-b1e0-a6deda18c342" :question/math-academy-id "q-72754" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/38e096b8d57404135b7fe3244e50989e66021f94bb2275c56956d8663575b4f8.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{85}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 23.908\\,7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & =112\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{37}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.403\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & =23.908\\,7+67.403\\,3 \\\\  & =91.311\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $91.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "203386bf-cdf1-5a78-92fd-36fababfb971" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5f0847-6dca-5e86-959a-5ce40230bdf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5f0847-6dca-5e86-959a-5ce40230bdf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261403} :answer-field/id #uuid "708fe7b0-f364-5757-a9a4-3709636c5143" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261402}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b55051a-8f37-5733-840a-d51d5d26cfae" :question/math-academy-id "q-73017" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!$ and $6!=6\\cdot 5!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $7!$ and $5!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9!\\text{ }5!}{7!\\text{ }6!} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7!\\cdot 5!}{7!\\cdot 6\\cdot 5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{6} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbcf8c96-eec1-510a-a0f6-919ab37fe0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261406} {:answer/id #uuid "65778dde-8bff-5b72-9171-0d6d29456876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-1)(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261407} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b3d8d-8d0a-5983-9a83-ba594f0c251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}^{2}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261408} {:answer/id #uuid "9a789b7d-babf-5062-84ac-2e74a8f93df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261409} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d2e29e-e230-52d1-892f-59e17d8cbfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d2e29e-e230-52d1-892f-59e17d8cbfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261410} :answer-field/id #uuid "72ddcd8c-501a-56c3-af42-4b7e20f51180" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261405}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c23c670-ac3c-5d23-92d6-e55e17344fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73023" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot \\cancel{(n-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-1)!}} \\\\  & =n(n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfa9af18-0b89-5af5-8a9c-58e05f56fcda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261423} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5e422b-412b-59e6-b5c3-c4c506500e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261424} {:answer/id #uuid "af294fe6-3ccd-5b35-81b4-8bfef70300b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261425} {:answer/id #uuid "09812225-edc5-5718-9382-e1d4c90dc8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261426} {:answer/id #uuid "3ada9d11-8263-5fb7-8095-302889d174e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ada9d11-8263-5fb7-8095-302889d174e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261427} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6ea9fe-5074-58f5-b881-5f4c8659a0cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261422}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "523c0634-c18b-5a9f-a160-daed53a38d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73028" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge -1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)!=(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n+1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(n+1)!}}{(n+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2673c1e-e300-569b-b7dd-0f49d98247c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi i}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2673c1e-e300-569b-b7dd-0f49d98247c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi i}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36b0235-d655-56f5-8e34-424c273be9f8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "645ac3a1-e76d-5129-98b6-1a939fdd45a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73184" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}(2x)$ between $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $5$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is  \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=5$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0}{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{20})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi i}{10}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd32a853-e0ec-5d56-bd6b-1c2e0e3249f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Formula for the Sum of a Geometric Series Given an Arbitrary Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f618185-e7f5-50de-b4d6-606378bd0ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({3}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f618185-e7f5-50de-b4d6-606378bd0ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({3}^{N}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261506} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4ca8f5-31d1-55cf-b634-394079caf97e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20f38d31-63e5-54d0-9319-23fda8e1e0ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-73426" :question/problem "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $3$rd term is $36$ and the common ratio is $3?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine ${a}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 36 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{(3-1)} \\\\ 36 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 36 & =9{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{{a}_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=4$ and $r=3.$ Substituting our values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{4(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(-2)}(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =-2(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & =2({3}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a83e174-ab83-5bf6-898e-0fe5cddc8ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for a Leg"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c86f5e68-c2d1-53bf-912a-da3c04143ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86f5e68-c2d1-53bf-912a-da3c04143ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261626} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a80ff8-d7ff-572a-b7fa-8b0c9739a31e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261625}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fd2571e-50e8-5420-9ce7-c41527aa46a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76560" :question/problem "In a right triangle $△XYZ,$ $∠X$ is a right angle, $XZ=\\sqrt{5}$ and $YZ=7.$ Find the cosine of $∠Y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ea0502e8b261358c7b55c60df02474c5b39a75cbb104d6ba3efb40300294ef56.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{XY}{7}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XY.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{Y{Z}^{2}-X{Z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}Y & =\\frac{XY}{7}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7782976-559c-51db-b8f9-00d346dba3ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Cubic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261666} {:answer/id #uuid "9e18b255-f768-5dae-b54d-2ae92a5196da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quadratic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261667} {:answer/id #uuid "8d38caf7-a6c9-5d09-b74b-d2b4b2412298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quartic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261668} {:answer/id #uuid "9473f7b6-a284-5fde-aeb0-9dfe495353c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261669} {:answer/id #uuid "15d3eddf-834d-5006-bbf7-cc0d476c9924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quintic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d38caf7-a6c9-5d09-b74b-d2b4b2412298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Quartic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261668} :answer-field/id #uuid "b750a845-1c7b-5d4f-a4ad-29faa7db7a8b" :answer-field/key "degree" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261665} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4b604e-7684-5565-8a86-33f164b9defa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Four or more terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261672} {:answer/id #uuid "9f58d42d-e476-5fab-a622-86cecad4798f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261673} {:answer/id #uuid "e72d8d82-9fba-5dc8-8296-9f13d453cba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261674} {:answer/id #uuid "ff74270a-5fc4-5dfa-b0bf-77e73a8f2ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff74270a-5fc4-5dfa-b0bf-77e73a8f2ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261675} :answer-field/id #uuid "d56f2c4a-4503-5de3-88ff-e2689ab3b2b2" :answer-field/key "terms" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a6f6828-8843-5046-833d-792745e1538f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77896" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $8{t}^{2}-5{t}^{4}$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has.\n\ndegree: {{degree}}\n\nterms: {{terms}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $8{t}^{2}-5{t}^{4}$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $4,$ so it is a quartic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quartic binomial."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8adb9e03-b9ff-5552-9ba6-2dd348547485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261690} {:answer/id #uuid "2d84c27a-5dc9-538b-a29d-15e59042e1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261691} {:answer/id #uuid "c628cb53-41cf-5b62-9e95-80cf1a678558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261692} {:answer/id #uuid "df46b48d-72ed-5615-8ae3-3775a22c0eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261693} {:answer/id #uuid "7c79bc7e-c0a1-5ada-92ce-10780a2dd723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261694} {:answer/id #uuid "28495b6b-7018-59ae-a633-b97302ca2b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261695} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b315a6-0eca-51f5-95ae-171c360d6e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261696} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe692cb-a210-5dd0-a29a-6c7703079da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe692cb-a210-5dd0-a29a-6c7703079da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261697} :answer-field/id #uuid "f669dac0-ac79-533e-b8d1-4325879d1d5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261689}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea098b9-ba41-5103-943b-e3ce0105dfe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78133" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60270e0c-a60c-5a5f-8993-3d8362081c8f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Common Logarithms Using a Calculator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee202b27-89f2-5d76-8c0b-be03fefdec59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee202b27-89f2-5d76-8c0b-be03fefdec59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261712} :answer-field/id #uuid "0451953f-c9c5-59ea-9f3d-e90993c1e47f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261711}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4700c93d-5a4c-5fb9-b8c3-61a4dc38cc19" :question/math-academy-id "q-78744" :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, find the value of $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(5x)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2x)}{x}$ at $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we replace $x=2$ and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =\\operatorname{log}(5\\cdot 2)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2\\cdot 2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}(10)-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the required value to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =1-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}(4)}{2} \\\\  & \\approx 1-\\frac{0.602\\,06}{2} \\\\  & \\approx 1-0.301\\,03 \\\\  & =0.698\\,97 \\\\  & \\approx 0.70\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8058df30-04de-555c-adfa-61fbf2b0ff31" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Rational Expressions in One Variable With Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "654ef566-689b-59dd-953c-167d1edb36fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7y-1}{2(2y-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654ef566-689b-59dd-953c-167d1edb36fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7y-1}{2(2y-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261756} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f15a93a-5243-54c1-8949-1842524c8b49" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c100db9-b510-53eb-9c1c-fbc265949ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-79547" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3y}{2y-1}=a$ and $\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)}=b,$ what is $a-b?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We wish to subtract the following fractions:\n\n$$\na-b=\\frac{3y}{2y-1}-\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)}\n$$\n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $2(2y-1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3y}{2y-1}-\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3y}{2y-1}-\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 3y}{2(2y-1)}-\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{2(2y-1)}-\\frac{1-y}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y-(1-y)}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y-1+y}{2(2y-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{7y-1}{2(2y-1)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f117fa8-c38a-5cf2-ab31-ccdda2449063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x<14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f117fa8-c38a-5cf2-ab31-ccdda2449063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\le x<14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261943} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b03df9e-7683-5b3c-8544-60ac14d86293" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d1da8f8-46b6-5144-a2e9-189ffbaad0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-87792" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3-2\\sqrt{2x+8}>-9.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2x+8} & >-12 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+8} & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(6).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x+8})}^{2} & <{6}^{2} \\\\ 2x+8 & <36 \\\\ 2x & <28 \\\\ x & <14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<14,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,14).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge -4,$ which can be expressed as $[-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,14)\\cap [-4,\\infty )=[-4,14)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [-4,14),$ which can also be written as $-4\\le x<14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c2518d5-0fde-5dfc-aa07-f1bdd3c1585a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the End-Behavior of a Transformed Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e352997-2274-5862-96fc-f97ff0c21c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e352997-2274-5862-96fc-f97ff0c21c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261982} :answer-field/id #uuid "a07160fd-6040-5369-9aa1-33db8f39241d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261981}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ed91a52-4c22-5f53-9a5a-98369b57e065" :question/math-academy-id "q-92957" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=4({5}^{-x}).$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4af39ad263d5fdeb3bf0a48e7306b24863fe464619218f84b4f053fdf6af3090.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the $x$-axis as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c89b5d7-043f-5ab8-992d-632e1f753871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c89b5d7-043f-5ab8-992d-632e1f753871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262126} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfbe259d-8da9-58c4-849d-95d31faeeb62" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c06f415-a866-5b9c-9b0b-66f9357ad393" :question/math-academy-id "q-98874" :question/problem "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}+4)-({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2})+4-x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bce21789-b6fb-5299-bf89-ace3e63e0ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce21789-b6fb-5299-bf89-ace3e63e0ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262129} :answer-field/id #uuid "849c3479-2585-5acb-aacb-109724b769f2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262128}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "956b6089-26d5-526f-9e16-af60f96d927f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98876" :question/problem "If $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-3$ and $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x+5,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2}-3)-(2{x}^{2}+3x+5) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2})-3x+(-3-5) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-3x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b514d4ef-68ad-5fd4-b802-e61ca86aec2c" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Equations With Radicals Into Logarithmic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a90a56-4e97-59c2-b288-0c141f96e1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{16}(2)=\\frac14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a90a56-4e97-59c2-b288-0c141f96e1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{16}(2)=\\frac14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262147} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ea54010-3788-5fb8-85bd-f50f6ab44cba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a272e9e-63cd-5eda-aedd-cff118d56832" :question/math-academy-id "q-99487" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[4]{16}=2$ is equivalent to\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n{16}^{1/4}=2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${16}^{1/4}=2$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=2,b=16$ and $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${log}_{16}2=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afb7f5bb-1a71-5bf5-a4a0-1354256be22b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ed6d89-2369-535d-945f-1639d7ec8515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ed6d89-2369-535d-945f-1639d7ec8515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262159} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc46136-de90-5e72-8301-b5f5942256c9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262158}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2160c283-c3ec-5a1f-9e6b-bbbe6f28e2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99705" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $100$ and $1\\,000$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & ={10}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,000 & ={10}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}(100)}-\\operatorname{log}(1\\,000)=\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{log}({10}^{2})}-\\operatorname{log}({10}^{3})=\\frac{7}{2}-3=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68bc143c-0ae4-5757-9301-c1143621dc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68bc143c-0ae4-5757-9301-c1143621dc0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262165} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cbaf4a-f7a5-5f3a-951b-c7f20d653587" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd2e08d3-b64d-5c1f-866d-cc3f34b11473" :question/math-academy-id "q-99719" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $0.000\\,01$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.000\\,01={10}^{-5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(0.000\\,01)=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-5.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-5})=-5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67d294d0-542a-5332-b837-256792817dde" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Common Logarithms of Fractions and Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fddb9305-d180-552f-8764-a6de81b573b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fddb9305-d180-552f-8764-a6de81b573b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262168} :answer-field/id #uuid "538406e0-44fb-5ffa-a87e-0c0b8a80f33e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262167}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c27c663-c4ba-5f94-a868-bd140e031303" :question/math-academy-id "q-99720" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}})=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute A Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a975cc-631d-5d9f-a650-71675ddb4917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a975cc-631d-5d9f-a650-71675ddb4917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262177} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a5bf6a-c62a-5cc2-9f6b-4f19779fd5b4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea0c4278-e87d-566a-b51b-71d40367ec17" :question/math-academy-id "q-99877" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4317726b2c9fbe22707377e1c713e8e8271ae95f0d818ac658da02cc81d64202.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x))={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2.$ \nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}({x}^{3}-5f(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}{x}^{3}-5\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}-5(2) \\\\  & =0-10 \\\\  & =-10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
